    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>According to Wovoka's teaching, as the anthropologist James Mooney recorded it, a five-day dance performed at the proper intervals would hasten the reunion of the living and the dead and bring a world without sickness, disease or old age.</li><li>The Lakota took up the dance in 1890 as the government cut their land and halved their rations; their version was directed towards millenarianism, an innovation that distinguished it from Wovoka's original teachings.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a>'s death on 15 December 1890 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wounded-knee-massacre">Wounded Knee Massacre</a> on 29 December, open practice declined, but the ceremony became clandestine and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caddo">Caddo</a> still practise it today.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A small set of Ghost Dance songs, curated by James Mooney, was recorded and pressed in 1894, soon after the commercialisation of Emile Berliner's Gramophone; the original masters are held at the Library of Congress.</p></aside>
    </section>

